.Dd July 23, 2026
.Dt XTC_ACCEL 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_accel_probe ,
.Nm xtc_accel_wait_fence ,
.Nm xtc_accel_run_blocking
.Nd park a fiber on a GPU/NPU completion; enumerate accelerators
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_accel.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_accel_probe "xtc_accel_dev_t *out" "int max" "int *out_n"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_accel_wait_fence "int fence_fd" "int64_t timeout_ns"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_accel_run_blocking "int (*fn)(void *)" "void *arg" "int *out_result"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An accelerator -- a GPU or an NPU (neural / vision processing unit,
also called a VPU) -- is, at the layer a concurrency runtime cares
about, an asynchronous I/O device: the caller submits an opaque
command buffer or compiled graph, the coprocessor runs it, and it
signals completion through a fence.  On Linux both device kinds are
DRM devices
.Pq Pa /dev/dri/renderD* No for a render GPU , Pa /dev/accel/accel* No for an NPU
whose completions are the same kernel object -- a
.Vt dma-fence
exported as a pollable
.Vt sync_file
file descriptor, the identical readiness event the loop already
multiplexes for sockets and pipes.  A GPU fence fd and an NPU fence fd
are indistinguishable to
.Xr poll 2 ,
so they are handled by one API here.
.Pp
The
.Nm xtc_accel
facility does exactly one thing: it parks the calling fiber on such a
completion and wakes it when the device is done.  It is
.Em not
a tensor library, a device-memory allocator, or a vendor-SDK
portability layer.
.Nm libxtc
links no GPU/NPU runtime
.Pq Xr Level Zero , CUDA , Vulkan , OpenVINO , ...\& :
the consumer links whichever runtime drives the device, submits the
work, and hands
.Nm
the resulting fence fd.  The abstraction stops at the fence; submission
and the compute itself live above it.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_accel_probe
enumerates the accelerator devices present on the host into
.Fa out Ns Bq 0 .. Ns Fa max ,
writing the total number found to
.Fa *out_n
(which may exceed
.Fa max
if the buffer was too small; only the first
.Fa max
are written).  Each
.Vt xtc_accel_dev_t
reports a
.Fa kind
.Pq Dv XTC_ACCEL_KIND_GPU No or Dv XTC_ACCEL_KIND_NPU ,
a short
.Fa name ,
the device
.Fa node
path, and the backing kernel
.Fa driver
name if cheaply known (for example
.Ql xe
or
.Ql intel_vpu ) .
These are for device selection and observability by the consumer and
its runtime;
.Nm libxtc
opens none of them.  The device kind is a tag only -- it never changes
how a fence is waited on.  Probing opens and submits nothing.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_accel_wait_fence
parks the calling fiber until the completion fence
.Fa fence_fd
signals or
.Fa timeout_ns
elapses
.Pq negative waits forever .
The fence fd is a pollable
.Vt sync_file
the consumer's runtime produced when it submitted work (from a
.Xr Level Zero
event or fence, an exported DRM sync object, a
.Dv VK_KHR_external_fence_fd
Vulkan fence, a CUDA event bridged to an
.Xr eventfd 2 ,
and so on).  While waiting the fiber holds no OS thread -- the wait
rides the loop's normal readiness path via
.Xr xtc_proc_wait_fd 3 .
.Nm libxtc
does not own, duplicate, or close
.Fa fence_fd ;
the caller retains ownership.  It must be called from within a fiber.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_accel_run_blocking
runs the synchronous accelerator call
.Fa fn Ns ( Ns Fa arg Ns )
on the
.Xr xtc_blocking 3
thread pool, parking the calling fiber until it returns and storing
.Fa fn Ns 's
return value in
.Fa *out_result
.Pq may be Dv NULL .
It is the fallback for runtimes that expose only a blocking
submit-and-wait with no pollable fence.  It holds one pool thread for
the duration of
.Fa fn ,
so it suits coarse-grained inference rather than thousands of tiny
operations per second; prefer
.Fn xtc_accel_wait_fence
when a fence fd is available.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_accel_probe
returns
.Dv XTC_OK
on success, including when zero devices are found (absence is not an
error), or
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
on a bad argument.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_accel_wait_fence
returns
.Dv XTC_OK
when the fence signals,
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
on timeout (the fence never fired),
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
on a bad file descriptor, or
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
when the library was built without accelerator support.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_accel_run_blocking
returns
.Dv XTC_OK
.Pq Fa fn No ran; see Fa *out_result ,
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a
.Dv NULL
.Fa fn ,
or an
.Xr xtc_blocking 3
error if the work could not be dispatched.  It works regardless of
whether accelerator support was built in.
.Sh AVAILABILITY
Accelerator support is selected at configure time with
.Fl -with-accel Ns = Ns Ar auto Ns | Ns Ar yes Ns | Ns Ar no
(default
.Ar auto :
enabled where the Linux DRM/accel subsystem is present).  When it is
not built in,
.Fn xtc_accel_probe
reports zero devices and
.Fn xtc_accel_wait_fence
returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS ,
following the same
.Dq always linkable, NOSYS when unsupported
convention as the TLS and cryptography modules;
.Fn xtc_accel_run_blocking
is unaffected.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_blocking 3 ,
.Xr xtc_proc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_io 3
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm xtc_accel
facility appeared in
.Nm libxtc
1.30.
